g one occurred gives no useful information about whether the other occurred","They must have equal probabilities","They must be complements"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9c74edc03c4eb40be2ac264e","time_limit":25},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $P(B)>0$, which condition is equivalent to independence?","options":["$P(A\\mid B)=P(B)$ always","$P(A\\mid B)=0$","$P(A\\mid B)=P(A)$","$P(A\\cup B)=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e8810c9c4bcfc4d4cc3409cf","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Are disjoint nonzero-probability events independent?","options":["No","Yes, always","Only if they have equal probabilities","Only if their union is empty"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d7ab168ec41ae81c91b7d9f","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $A$ and $B$ are independent, are $A$ and $B^c$ independent?","options":["Yes","No, never","Only if $P(B)=1/2$","Only if they are disjoint"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba264e2cd04b34ac518be153","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Two separate fair coin tosses landing heads are what kind of events?","options":["Disjoint events","Identical events","Independent events","Impossible events (probability zero)"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9ab7ee7fed4ca4ae6311a6c1","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Drawing two cards without replacement usually creates what?","options":["Dependent events","Independent events","Complementary events only","Events with no probabilities"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3ff0f8bb4714cbf1a1d1ccbf","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.5$, $P(B)=0.4$, and $A,B$ are independent, what is $P(A\\cap B)$?","options":["$0.9$","$0.1$","$0.5$","$0.2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fd2abf3439a9abb6d1898d8a","time_limit":25},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Rolling a die and flipping a coin under the usual product model are what?","options":["Disjoint","The same event","Undefined","Independent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4a96f59ae553a474ab575e33","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.3$ and $P(A\\mid B)=0.3$ with $P(B)>0$, what does this suggest?","options":["$A$ and $B$ must be disjoint","$A$ must be a null event with probability $0$","$A$ and $B$ may be independent","$A$ is the complement of $B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2f10110d19df9c76107ef390","time_limit":35},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is independence different from disjointness?","options":["Independence and disjointness are just two different names for exactly the same condition","Independence requires empty intersection","Independence means no probability change; disjointness means no simultaneous occurrence","Disjointness requires product probabilities"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"962724de8e88f845a3a95e49","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For independent events $A,B$, what is $P(A\\cup B)$?","options":["$P(A)+P(B)-P(A)P(B)$","$P(A)P(B)$","$P(A)+P(B)$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7adda42ce324dd7152bdc44f","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_013","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which conditions define mutual independence of three events $A,B,C$?","options":["every subset of them factors as a product of probabilities","only the full triple intersection factors, not the pairwise ones","they are pairwise disjoint (cannot co-occur)","each event has probability exactly $1/3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af01e0bc06d1d5af4bb41e50","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.2$, $P(B)=0.5$, and $P(A\\cap B)=0.1$, are $A$ and $B$ independent?","options":["No, because the events overlap","No, because $P(A)\\ne P(B)$","Yes, because all events are independent","Yes, because $0.2\\cdot0.5=0.1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"22521f79e51058028b60978b","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $P(A)=0.4$, $P(B)=0.5$, and $P(A\\cap B)=0.3$, are the events independent?","options":["No, because $0.4\\cdot0.5=0.2\\ne0.3$","Yes, because $P(A\\cap B)>0$","Yes, because $P(A)<P(B)$","No, because independent events must be disjoint"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1a39dcd1340191e90609d24b","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_016","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can an event be independent of itself?","options":["Only if $P(A)=0$ or $P(A)=1$","Always","Never","Only if $P(A)=1/2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"611ca0b2fc633a5744d7eb16","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why do disjoint events with positive probabilities fail independence?","options":["The union probability is always zero","They must have equal probabilities","The conditional probabilities are undefined for every event","Intersection probability is $0$, but $P(A)P(B)$ is positive"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"50faca59f04f7c0787e03bee","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $P(A)=0$, what happens to the factorization condition with any event $B$?","options":["It becomes $P(A\\cap B)=0=P(A)P(B)$","It becomes impossible because $A$ cannot be an event","It forces $P(B)=0$","It implies $A=S$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fbaeb3f1da005ed584ae3c23","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Two fair coins are tossed. Let $A$ be first coin heads and $B$ be second coin heads. What is $P(A\\cap B)$?","options":["$1/2$","$0$","$1/4$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"961453e87399a911911c0df1","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch4_independent_events_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"One card is drawn. Are 'red card' and 'black card' independent?","options":["Yes, because their probabilities are equal","Yes, because every card has one color","No, because they are identical events","No, they are disjoint with positive probabilities"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c6700300baeb25cb98428dbf","time_limit":40}]}}